  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1017 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThe borderland region will be trending toward a mostly dry pattern\nand warmer temperatures the next couple of days as high pressure \nbuilds aloft over the area. While sufficient moisture remains, \nwarmer temperatures aloft will increase stability which will limit\nthunderstorm development to mainly mountains the next few days, \nwhere orographic list can aid in storm development. The lowlands \nwill remain mostly dry but may see a a brief isolated storm with \nspotty rainfall. Day time temperatures will run above normal \nthrough the weekend with the lowland deserts seeing values \napproaching the upper 90s to near 100 by the weekend. \n\nThe weather pattern will become more active early next week as the\nhigh shifts to the east in response to a large low pressure system\nmoving inland from the Pacific northwest. This will set up a moist\nsoutherly flow aloft that will facilitate widespread thunderstorm\ndevelopment across the area for several days. Increased moisture\nand rain chances will result in lower temperatures closer to\nseasonal averages. \n\nThe long term outlook for late next week remains uncertain with\nlong range forecast models split on whether the moisture and\nthunderstorm chances continue or we trend back to a drier weather\npattern.",
